::: {.doc-subject}

Add a wheel graph to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L8868-L8983){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_wheel_graph(
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Wheel graph](../assets/graphs/add-wheel-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three wheel graphs side by side for n=4, 5, and 7: a hub in the middle joined to every node of a rim cycle of three, four, and six nodes. The hub has n - 1 neighbors and every rim node has three." width="100%"}

Creates a hub node joined to every node of a cycle of `n - 1` rim nodes. The result has
`n` nodes and `2(n - 1)` edges: `n - 1` spokes plus `n - 1` rim edges. The hub has
degree `n - 1` and every rim node degree `3`. A wheel is a star whose spokes are joined
into a ring, or equivalently a cycle with one extra node connected to everything.

The hub is created first and the rim nodes follow it in order around the cycle. In a
directed graph the spokes point outward from the hub and the rim runs in one direction
(each rim node to the next, the last back to the first). The wheel is appended without
touching or connecting to existing nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a
single step.

Wheel graphs are useful for:

- modeling a central coordinator plus a ring of peers that also talk to their neighbors
  (a hub-and-ring network)
- test cases for centrality, where the hub dominates but rim nodes are not leaves
- graph theory examples: wheels are planar, 3-connected, and self-dual, and `n=4` gives
  the complete graph `K4`
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Total number of nodes, counting the hub (so the rim has `n - 1` nodes). Must be at
    least `4`, so that the rim is at least a triangle.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; the hub
    gets the first number. A label that is already used raises `ValueError` before
    anything is added. If `False`, the nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge (spokes and rim
    alike). If `None` (default), no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` is less than `4`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_star_graph`) 

:   Add a star graph.

[](`~add_cycle_graph`) 

:   Add a cycle graph.

[](`~layout_shell`) 

:   Place the nodes on concentric circles.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A wheel of `n` nodes has a hub, the first node, and a rim cycle of the other `n - 1`, with a
spoke from the hub to each node of the rim. The hub's degree is `n - 1`, and each rim node
has its two rim neighbors and the hub:

::: {#7a3fac0c .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_wheel_graph(n=6)

graph.count_edges(), graph.get_node_df()["degree"].to_list()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(10, [5, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3])
```
:::
:::


### The smallest wheel and a larger one

The smallest wheel, with `n=4` on the left, is the complete graph on four nodes. With `n=9`
on the right, the rim has eight nodes:

::: {#a5f5ee7b .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
small = ec.Graph().add_wheel_graph(n=4)

small.is_isomorphic(ec.Graph().add_full_graph(n=4))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
True
```
:::
:::


::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#245c114f .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
small.layout_shell(nlist=[[1], [2, 3, 4]]).plot(
    layout="pos", label_by="label", width=320, height=320, legend=False
)
```

